{
  "dom_id": "step-e10040",
  "name": "Example: Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications",
  "problem": "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $60 \\, \\textrm{mph}$ on a standard test car.   A sample of $81$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $132\\, \\textrm{ ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}.$ Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu$ of these tires.    \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2.$ However, the sample size $n = 81 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm  t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=132 \\, \\textrm{ft}, \\qquad\nn = 81, \\qquad\ns = 5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value $t_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T > t_{80, 0.025}) = 0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14188481/assets/d513fbeee39a42a5e897c1b171d7e820196066517c5aa1c6c12549a98ce00ddd.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that $t_{80,0.025}=1.990{:}$ \n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}80}$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.990}$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}132 & \\pm 1.99\\cdot \\frac{5.4}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 132 & \\pm 1.194\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
